Here are a few pointers:

1) Get an early superpet - either by polymorph or by a figurine.
2) Get an early magic whistle (or blessed leaf)
3) Self-Polymorph - It is MUCH easier as an arch-lich or xorn.
   As a xorn, you can spend most of your time in rock where
   little can attack you.
4) Eat charged rings of protection - the only way to boost your AC.
5) Carry a half-damage quest artifact. Doubles effective hit points.
6) Carry a magic resistance quest artifact, or wield Magicbane.
7) If you play Slashem instead of nethack, you can use shop services
   to provide many of the functions that normally require scrolls,
   spells, or altars.
8) If you play Slashem, gypsies can be used to get wishes.
9) Scrolls of scare monster are very useful.
   Keep a set of blessed and uncursed, use the blessed, re-bless.
10) As an atheist pacifist, alignment is a major problem.
    The surest way to boost your alignment enough to go on the
    quest is by angering a peaceful priest not of your alignment,
    then using a magic harp to calm him down.

94 wishes was a bit excessive.  I didn't bother to look for ANY useful
items, and often my potions of full healing and paralysis and scrolls
of scare monster were destroyed.  If I had been specifically trying to
minimize the number of wishes used, I probably could have managed with
only 40 or so, especially if I were willing to forgo bagless conduct.

Although the obvious use of the loadstone bug is infinite wishes, it
also allows you to duplicate artifacts, as well as overenchanted
armor, weapons, and rings. By abusing the loadstone bug, one can
easily have an army of 8 superpets, all wearing +8 armor in every slot
and all wielding the +10 or better Grayswandir. Chowing down on
unlimited +7 rings of increase damage can result in a character that
can toss a rock at any supermonster and do over 120 HP of damage.  I
was actually tempted to eat negatively charged rings of ID in my game
to avoid the risk of accidentally breaking pacifism.

